      "content": "The sudden influx of a distinct cultural group often triggers a cycle of initial apprehension and perceived threat regarding established neighborhood norms, which can temporarily fray existing social cohesion. This dynamic frequently manifests as moral panics or visible tensions, as residents grappling with safety concerns or economic displacement may clash with new arrivals regarding shared public spaces and values. To effectively model this in your policy paper, acknowledge these friction points as a natural, albeit painful, transitional phase that, if managed with transparent communication and community engagement initiatives, can evolve into a more complex and integrated social fabric. Ultimately, social harmony in such contexts depends less on the mere percentage of group presence and more on the institutional ability to facilitate dialogue and mutual adaptation.",
